n  that has been led by the west is really  working in their favor going back to  ukraine before we move on  uh the prime minister's view  is that ukraine shouldn't be coerced  into what he would describe as a bad  piece and he's worried that  the economic fallout of this war  might compel other countries to now  try and force  or encourage ukraine into  a piece in which perhaps some of the  land is annexed what's your view of that  do you agree with boris johnson on that  i i agree with him about the importance  of making sure  that we do everything in our power to  protect the sovereignty of ukraine what  i think however is is missing here is is  the greater coordination in policy now  there's been a huge amount of  coordination in terms of  the first round of sanctions there's  been a huge amount of coordination in  terms of saying we'll give you this or  that military support i think boris  johnson perhaps should spend more time  trying to bring people together to agree  on what the objectives are for the next  stage of this of this enterprise and of  course we want to support ukraine and of  course there is a there is there is an  issue about where you move from a  defensive position that is you're  helping ukraine defend itself to what  russia would see as aggression uh but  these are things that i think we've got  to discuss together and i think  there is a danger that america britain  germany france italy all go in different  directions over the next year  kierstama he  is the labour leader obviously  the question i want to ask you is do you  think he sealed the deal with the  british public  in that  some of his colleagues as you will know  have sort of criticized kierstarma for  being too boring to quote them  um  it's kind of a bit true he does does he  come across as a bit boring a bit  loyally  not a charismatic leader i think every  question i think every criticism that  has been visited on uh kiestama was  visited on me tony blair jim calligan  uh harry people didn't call tony blair  boring did they yeah but they they did  say that labour didn't have an exciting  enough agenda at certain points i mean  you remember in 1997 people said it was  too modest and and everything else but  all these criticisms are thrown at labor  leaders and and they're mainly thrown of  course by a conservative press that's  gotten interested in defeating the  labour party uh my advice to to give  someone is usually not to take my advice  because it's better that he does his own  thing but my advice to him has always  been not to l not to listen to this and  to get on with the job that he said  himself one one to to prepare the party  so that it's seen by the public as a  national party that's capable of  representing the whole country secondly  to develop his policy  and third to put across his policy to as  wide a group of people as possible and  that includes talking to no voters in  scotland  as yes voters as well as no voters in  scotland and it includes talking to  lead voters as well as remain voters in  england we've got to find a way of  bringing this country together and i  think kia stammer is better placed to do  that than boris johnson do you think the  prime minister lacks integrity clearly  uh he  he he has broken the law uh clearly he  has uh has failed to follow the rules  fairly fairly he's evaded his own  ministerial code all these things are  true and i think he's admitted that to  be the case the question i think  that he didn't quite answer at the  weekend is whether he's going to change  would you have resigned if you were him  at this point well i couldn't i'll look  i'll be honest i could not have imagined  myself  breaking  the law in downing street during during  a a crisis like like we've had and  therefore i i i could not have stayed in  office if i thought i'd let people down  in that way i've got to ask you this  scottish independence do you think that  there will be a second referendum in  your lifetime or my lifetime  i i wouldn't rule out that but i would  say that scotland deserves better than  what's happening at the moment i mean  we're in a endless debate about  constitutional details that is leading  us nowhere  i would like to see and i would like to  put forward a far more ambitious plan  for the future of scotland one that  solves some of the constitutional  difficulties deals with the economic  challenges we face  builds a far better social fabric for  this country and that's really the issue  for the people of scotland if the issue  is posed as change  through independence versus no change  and the only alternative is independence  then independence will will will be  something that people would be prepared  to consider  but if it's one kind of change for  scotland staying within britain but in a  better situation both in terms of its  economic  social and political future  as against independence then the change  that is within britain will win  do you think kirsten will be the next  prime minister of this country yes i do  do you really yes i do  okay thank you thanks for your time
 

